## 🔎 Research objective

EvidenceLoop investigates how repository evidence and execution feedback affect the generation and prioritization of regression tests. For each benchmark task, the model receives the buggy repository state, changed source, relevant tests, direct callers, and an optional public bug summary. The fixed revision is withheld from generation and used only by the evaluator.

The primary outcome is an **oracle match**: a candidate test fails on the buggy revision and passes on the fixed revision. Collection errors, infrastructure failures, timeouts, and unrelated execution errors are recorded separately and do not constitute detections.

## ⚙️ Workflow

1. Check out the buggy revision and identify changed files.
2. Collect changed-source evidence, related tests, and direct callers.
3. Generate focused pytest code using the configured model.
4. Extract code blocks, validate Python syntax, and make one repair request when output is invalid or truncated.
5. Run the candidate against buggy and fixed revisions in the task’s Docker executor.
6. Score the result using the behavioral oracle and retain the code, evidence, model diagnostics, token usage, and execution results in JSON.

The comparison runner implements five conditions:

| Condition          | Method                                                           |
| ------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `existing_tests` | Run the task’s baseline test on buggy and fixed revisions.      |
| `one_shot`       | Generate from changed source and the bug summary.                |
| `retrieval`      | Add related tests and collected repository evidence.             |
| `feedback`       | Refine a candidate once using buggy-revision execution feedback. |
| `full`           | Run the EvidenceLoop workflow and evaluate candidate ranking.    |

## 📊 Evaluation results

### Initial frozen comparison

The initial comparison used prompt v1, two benchmark tasks, and 10 repetitions per task: 20 runs per condition and 80 LLM-condition runs overall. The table reports valid candidate runs and oracle matches. Generation failures are included as non-detections in the intention-to-treat rates.

| Condition         | Valid candidate runs | Oracle matches | Match rate |
| ----------------- | -------------------: | -------------: | ---------: |
| Existing tests    |                   — |        10 / 20 |        50% |
| One-shot          |               2 / 20 |         1 / 20 |         5% |
| Retrieval         |               2 / 20 |         1 / 20 |         5% |
| Feedback          |               0 / 20 |         0 / 20 |         0% |
| Full EvidenceLoop |               0 / 20 |         0 / 20 |         0% |

The existing test detected the Black defect in 10 of 10 repetitions and the FastAPI defect in 0 of 10. One-shot generation detected FastAPI once; retrieval detected Black once. Feedback and full EvidenceLoop did not yield valid candidates in this run, so their results do not measure refinement or ranking effectiveness. The artifact records generation and provider errors alongside these outcomes.

### Post-fix functional validation

After updating the prompt, code-fence extraction, syntax repair, and Gemini thinking configuration, a one-repetition Black validation produced syntactically valid candidates in all four LLM conditions. Every model response completed with `STOP` rather than `MAX_TOKENS`.

| Condition         |      Valid candidates | Oracle match |
| ----------------- | --------------------: | -----------: |
| One-shot          |                     1 |           No |
| Retrieval         |                     1 |           No |
| Feedback          | 1 initial + 1 refined |          Yes |
| Full EvidenceLoop |                     1 |          Yes |

This validation checks that the generation and execution paths work after the changes. With one task and one repetition, it is an implementation check rather than a comparative performance estimate.

