## What caused the poor results

The summary’s “76 failures” combines **two different problems**:

- **60 provider failures:** the LLM client raised `RuntimeError` after its retries. That is 60 of 80 generation conditions.
- **16 invalid completions:** the provider returned text, but it was not valid Python. The generator rejected it during parsing.
- **4 valid candidates:** only 2 passed the buggy/fixed oracle.

The exact cause of the 60 provider failures **can’t be recovered from these artifacts**. [llm.py](/E:/evidence-loop/src/evidenceloop/llm.py) replaces the provider’s underlying exception with a generic message, and the comparison artifact saves only that message. Rate or token limits are plausible: successful calls used up to 12,169 input tokens, and Google documents per-minute request and token limits plus `429` quota errors. Its guidance recommends exponential backoff for transient failures. But without the original status and message, I can’t say these were definitely `429`s. [Google’s rate limit documentation](https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/rate-limits), [troubleshooting guidance](https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/troubleshooting)

For the 16 invalid completions, the prompt asks for Python, but the code accepts only responses that parse immediately; it doesn’t repair or retry a response-format failure. One observed response was conversational prose rather than a test. That is enough to explain those parse failures, though the raw completions for the rest were not saved. See [prompts.py](/E:/evidence-loop/src/evidenceloop/prompts.py) and [generator.py](/E:/evidence-loop/src/evidenceloop/generator.py).

**Feedback and full conditions never reached refinement or useful ranking.** Both need an initial candidate that can be parsed and executed. With no usable initial candidates, the run had no behavioral feedback to refine and nothing meaningful to rank.

The few valid candidates show a separate test-quality issue: Black’s one-shot candidate tested formatting and passed on both revisions; another Black candidate failed on the fixed revision too. Only the Black retrieval candidate and FastAPI one-shot candidate distinguished buggy from fixed.

One correction to my earlier update: **$16 was an estimate enforced by the runner’s preflight check, not a hard API-spend cap.** The reports don’t contain complete usage or provider pricing for failed calls, so actual spend can’t be calculated from them.

The experiment shows that the pipeline was unreliable under this run, but it does **not** isolate whether the provider failures came from rate limits, quota, network errors, or another API problem. The runner discarded the evidence needed to tell. The next useful step is to preserve provider status/messages and token usage on every failed call, then do a single controlled generation attempt after checking the provider’s active limits.

> which model should we use to test generation (we are using gemini 3.8 which is text out model) is it fine are if there are specific models we should use?

<details><summary>2 previous messages</summary>

> I’ll check the current provider documentation and distinguish model suitability from the API failures in the run. A text output model is generally the right modality for producing Python tests; the more important choices are code-generation capability, output constraints, and rate limits.
>
> <details><summary>Searched the web</summary>
>
> Searched the web for `site:ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/models/gemini-3.8-flash Gemini 3.8 Flash code generation structured output ...`
>
> Searched the web for `site:ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/models/gemini-4-argon stable model Gemini 4 Argon ...`
>
> </details>

</details>

Yes—**text output is the right modality** for this task. The model needs to return Python source, and Gemini 3.8 Flash supports text output, structured outputs, and software engineering tasks. The model choice itself does not explain the experiment’s failures: many runs hit generic provider errors, and others returned text that the parser rejected. [Gemini 3.8 Flash documentation](https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/models/gemini-3.8-flash)

My recommendation:

- **Keep `gemini-3.8-flash` as the primary model** for the next controlled run. It is a stable model, and keeping it fixed lets us diagnose the API and response-format problems without changing another variable.
- **Try `gemini-4-argon` as a separate comparison arm** if you want to measure whether a stronger reasoning model produces better tests. Google currently describes it as its flagship model for complex software engineering. It is a distinct, more expensive model, so record it as a separate experimental condition. [Gemini 4 Argon documentation](https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/models/gemini-4-argon) [Pricing](https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/pricing)

For either model, constrain the response to a structured format containing test-code strings, then parse and syntax-check each test. That directly addresses the prose and invalid-Python outputs we observed. It won’t fix API quota or network failures, which need the original provider error details and proper backoff.

